  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nFog potential this morning, gradual increase in warmth/moisture\n\nCirrus from convection over North Dakota will continue to spill\ninto the area early this morning. This complicates the forecast\nfor potential fog development in valleys and low-lying areas, \nin what would otherwise be a decent synoptic setup with high \npressure overhead. But evening HRRR runs trended toward some \nbreezier winds of 10-15 knots in the 500-1500 ft layer. All this\nsuggesting that fog may be more patchy this morning. \n\nThere is an outside chance some decaying convective showers \nfrom the Northern Plains would be steered southeastward in the \nupper flow to clip areas west of the Mississippi River with some\nshowers this afternoon. Several 24.00Z CAMs are showing this, \nbut the HRRR is mainly dry.\n\nDaytime highs will be warming a bit today into midweek as \nsoutherly return flow develops. Moisture will also gradually be \non the increase ahead of a pronounced upper shortwave trough \nprogged to cut across northern Minnesota/Wisconsin toward \nWednesday. Dewpoints will rise back into the 60s, and with a \nfeed of Gulf moisture there is a >70% likelihood for \nprecipitable water to climb above 1.25 inches by Tuesday \nafternoon. \n\nStorm chances on Tuesday, some could be strong\n\nStrong storm chances on Tuesday will hinge on magnitude of \ninstability and its ramifications on how robust updrafts can \nbecome. GEFS 23.18Z probabilities for CAPE >1000 J/kg range from\nabout 60-80% west of the Mississippi River to 50% or less in \nwestern Wisconsin. This was a slight decrease from the 12Z run \nas models are grappling with a potential EML and less favorable \nmid-level lapse rates. Capping and instability profiles will be \nmost favorable for convection over our northwestern counties. \nThat's also where favorable deep layer shear is most likely, \nwith elongated hodographs perpendicular to an approaching cold \nfront. Thus, it bears watching how all these elements will \nultimately come together Tuesday afternoon-evening, with some \nstrong to possibly severe storms looking to be a possibility. \n\nRest of the week\n\nCyclonic flow aloft persists into Wednesday which could foster \nsome diurnal shower activity. The upper shortwave departs by \nThursday with 850mb temps decreasing slightly overhead through \nFriday. The overall pattern shifts heading into next weekend as \nwarm air advection ramps up. This will support a return of \nrain/storm chances but details are pretty murky that far out.",
